      "problem": "If $a={\\text{i}}^{7},$ then ${a}^{2}=$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the power law for exponents, we have\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}={({\\text{i}}^{7})}^{2}={\\text{i}}^{7\\cdot 2}={\\text{i}}^{14}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\text{i}$ raised to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4,$ and raise $\\text{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=14.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n14÷4=3\\,\\text{R}2.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{i}}^{14}={\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.\n$$",
